.TH TIMEOUT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
timeout \- run a command with a bounded running time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timeout
.RB [ \-s
.IR signal ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR duration ]
.I duration utility
.RI [ argument .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timeout
runs
.I utility
(with any given
.I argument
operands) as a separate process. If
.I utility
is still running after the first
.I duration
operand has elapsed,
.B timeout
sends it
.I signal
(SIGTERM, by default) as its first attempt to stop it. If
.B \-k
was given and
.I utility
is still running its own
.I duration
after that,
.B timeout
escalates to sending it SIGKILL.
.PP
A
.I duration
of zero, for either the main
.I duration
operand or
.BR \-k 's,
disables the associated timeout entirely: a main
.I duration
of zero means
.I utility
simply runs to completion with no deadline at all (equivalent to
running it directly, with no time limit); a
.B \-k
.I duration
of zero is equivalent to not giving
.B \-k
at all.
.SH "DURATION SYNTAX"
A
.I duration
is a (possibly fractional) decimal number, optionally followed by a
single unit suffix:
.B s
for seconds (the default when no suffix is given),
.B m
for minutes,
.B h
for hours, or
.B d
for days.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " signal"
Send
.I signal
instead of the default SIGTERM when the main
.I duration
elapses.
.I signal
may be given as a name, matched case-insensitively and with or
without a leading
.BR SIG
(e.g.
.BR KILL ,
.BR sigkill ,
or
.BR SigKill ),
or as a plain decimal signal number.
.TP
.BI \-k " duration"
If
.I utility
is still running
.I duration
after the first signal was sent, send it SIGKILL as well.
.PP
There is no long-option spelling for either
.B \-s
or
.BR \-k ,
and no equivalent of a
.BR \-\-foreground ,
.BR \-\-preserve\-status ,
or
.B \-\-verbose
option found in some other implementations of
.BR timeout ;
none of that behavior is implemented, and the exit-status convention
below is always used regardless of how
.I utility
was actually stopped.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I duration
The main timeout, in the syntax described above.
.TP
.I utility
The command to run.
.TP
.I argument
Arguments to pass to
.IR utility .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 124
.I utility
was still running when
.I duration
elapsed and was signaled as a result (and was not, in the end, stopped
by the KILL(9) signal itself \(em see 137 below).
.TP
.B 125
.B timeout
itself failed: an invalid option, a malformed
.I duration
or
.IR signal ,
or an internal failure while waiting for
.IR utility .
.TP
.B 126
.I utility
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I utility
could not be found.
.TP
.B 137
.I utility
(or
.B timeout
itself) was stopped by the KILL(9) signal, whether because
.BR \-k 's
escalation fired, or because
.B \-s
was given KILL (or an equivalent signal number) as the first signal
and that is what actually stopped
.IR utility .
.TP
.I otherwise
.IR utility 's
own exit status, if it exited on its own before any timeout applied
(or
.RI ( 128 + n )
if it was terminated by a signal
.I n
on its own, unrelated to any timeout escalation).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR time (1)
